# TTP-2026-0130 — AI SOC Triage Discrimination Under Decoy Noise — True-Positive Elevation + False-Positive Suppression (SIM-MP-015)
# XQL paste-buffer generated by detection_scanner/scripts/export_artifacts.py

## ABIOC — MP-015 Impossible-Travel Privileged Sign-In (behavioral-ML, causality-anchored)
# severity: high
# behavioral_profile: machine-learning
# causality_anchor: Learned per-principal sign-in-geography baseline -> a privileged success from a second country inside one login window that diverges from that baseline, anchored to the originating identity and emitted as a shared normalized_user (UPN local-part) so the endpoint deviations can be equi-joined to it across planes.
# A privileged principal authenticates successfully from two distinct geographies inside a single short login window — physically impossible travel. The ABIOC anchors the deviation to the account's learned sign-in-geography baseline, not to any single bad IP, so it fires on the first genuinely anomalous privileged session rather than on a static allow/deny list. This is malicious signal 1 of 3 in the identity-driven chain.

dataset = msft_azure_ad_signin
| filter auth_result = "success"
| filter user_type = "privileged" or is_privileged = true
| comp count_distinct(location_country) as countries, count_distinct(source_ip) as ips, min(_time) as first_seen, max(_time) as last_seen by user_principal_name
| filter countries >= 2
| alter travel_window_seconds = last_seen - first_seen
| alter normalized_user = lowercase(arrayindex(split(user_principal_name, "@"), 0))
| filter travel_window_seconds <= 3600
| fields user_principal_name, normalized_user, countries, ips, travel_window_seconds

## ABIOC — MP-015 Abnormal PowerShell Execution Chain from Office Parent (behavioral-ML, causality-anchored)
# severity: high
# behavioral_profile: machine-learning
# causality_anchor: Compromised-principal session -> Office/scripting parent (the winword.exe CGO) -> encoded/downloading PowerShell child, an anomalous process lineage keyed on causality_actor_process_image_name (the Causality Group Owner the CAE blames, not merely the immediate actor) and stamped with causality_id so every raw event in the chain shares one join key. Emitted as the same normalized_user (domain-stripped effective username) so it equi-joins the identity-plane sign-in across planes.
# An Office or scripting-host parent spawns PowerShell with encoded, no-profile, or download-cradle arguments — the parent-child lineage a benign PowerShell session never exhibits. The ABIOC fires on the abnormal process-chain shape under the compromised principal, not on the PowerShell binary itself (which is legitimate). This is malicious signal 2 of 3, anchored to the same principal as the impossible-travel sign-in.

dataset = xdr_data
| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS and event_sub_type = ENUM.PROCESS_START
| filter action_process_image_name in ("powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe")
| filter causality_actor_process_image_name in ("winword.exe", "excel.exe", "outlook.exe", "wscript.exe", "mshta.exe") or actor_process_image_name in ("winword.exe", "excel.exe", "outlook.exe", "wscript.exe", "mshta.exe", "cmd.exe")
| filter action_process_command_line contains "-enc" or action_process_command_line contains "-EncodedCommand" or action_process_command_line contains "DownloadString" or action_process_command_line contains "IEX" or action_process_command_line contains "-nop"
| alter normalized_user = lowercase(arrayindex(split(actor_effective_username, "\\"), -1))
| comp count() as suspicious_ps by agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, normalized_user, causality_actor_process_image_name, causality_id
| filter suspicious_ps >= 1
| fields agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, normalized_user, causality_actor_process_image_name, causality_id, suspicious_ps

## ABIOC — MP-015 Rapid Privilege Escalation Within Minutes of First Access (behavioral-ML, causality-anchored)
# severity: high
# behavioral_profile: machine-learning
# causality_anchor: Impossible-travel privileged sign-in (identity plane) -> admin-group-membership add / integrity transition (endpoint plane) equi-joined on normalized_user AND anchored to causality_actor_process_image_name + causality_id (the same winword.exe CGO chain that spawned the abnormal PowerShell), with the access->elevation delta bounded to <= 900s. The escalation is therefore provably lineage-linked to the same principal's originating access AND to the same process causality chain, not a standalone elevated process time-coincident with the sign-in.
# The same principal performs an escalation ACTION — adding itself to the local administrators group (net localgroup administrators ... /add, Add-LocalGroupMember) or a medium->high process-integrity TRANSITION — within minutes of the impossible-travel sign-in. The ABIOC keys on the escalation action (not on steady-state high-integrity/root processes, which are the admin/daemon norm on every host) and joins the endpoint event to the identity-plane sign-in on the shared normalized_user, asserting the access->elevation delta is <= 900s. Firing therefore requires the rapid, anomalous escalation the name claims — not a bare elevated process. This is malicious signal 3 of 3.

dataset = xdr_data
| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS and event_sub_type = ENUM.PROCESS_START
| filter (action_process_command_line contains "net localgroup administrators" and action_process_command_line contains "/add") or action_process_command_line contains "Add-LocalGroupMember" or (actor_process_integrity_level = "medium" and action_process_integrity_level = "high")
| alter normalized_user = lowercase(arrayindex(split(actor_effective_username, "\\"), -1))
| comp min(_time) as first_elevation, count() as elevation_actions by agent_hostname, normalized_user, causality_actor_process_image_name, causality_id
| join type = inner (dataset = msft_azure_ad_signin | filter auth_result = "success" | filter user_type = "privileged" or is_privileged = true | alter normalized_user = lowercase(arrayindex(split(user_principal_name, "@"), 0)) | comp min(_time) as signin_time by normalized_user) as signin on normalized_user = signin.normalized_user
| alter access_to_elevation_seconds = first_elevation - signin.signin_time
| filter access_to_elevation_seconds >= 0 and access_to_elevation_seconds <= 900
| fields agent_hostname, normalized_user, causality_actor_process_image_name, causality_id, signin.signin_time, first_elevation, access_to_elevation_seconds, elevation_actions

## VALIDATION — MP-015 Malicious Endpoint Chain Present for Compromised Principal
# purpose: validation
# dataset: xdr_data

dataset = xdr_data
| filter _time > to_timestamp(current_time() - 3600)
| filter event_type = ENUM.PROCESS and event_sub_type = ENUM.PROCESS_START
| filter action_process_image_name in ("powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe") or (action_process_command_line contains "net localgroup administrators" and action_process_command_line contains "/add") or action_process_command_line contains "Add-LocalGroupMember"
| comp count_distinct(action_process_image_name) as malicious_stages, count() as events by agent_hostname, actor_effective_username
| filter malicious_stages >= 1
| fields agent_hostname, actor_effective_username, malicious_stages, events

